WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:02.035
Breaking this morning, First

00:00:02.035 --> 00:00:03.870
Republic Bank has become

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:06.706
the second largest bank failure in U.S.

00:00:06.706 --> 00:00:07.073
history.

00:00:07.073 --> 00:00:09.275
Regulators have seized the bank,

00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:10.910
sold off most of its assets

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:12.012
to JPMorgan Chase

00:00:12.012 --> 00:00:13.747
in a rescue deal as America's

00:00:13.747 --> 00:00:15.949
banking crisis flares up again.

00:00:15.949 --> 00:00:16.516
In a statement

00:00:16.516 --> 00:00:17.851
this morning, JPMorgan Chase's

00:00:17.851 --> 00:00:18.418
CEO Jamie

00:00:18.418 --> 00:00:19.519
Dimon says Our government

00:00:19.519 --> 00:00:21.488
invited us and others to step up

00:00:21.488 --> 00:00:22.589
and we did.

00:00:22.589 --> 00:00:23.023
He didn't know

00:00:23.023 --> 00:00:25.625
if this will benefit JPMorgan Chase.

00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:26.760
We'll talk a lot more about it.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:27.427
But look, First

00:00:27.427 --> 00:00:28.795
Republic is now the third

00:00:28.795 --> 00:00:30.964
major bank to fail. Since March.

00:00:30.964 --> 00:00:32.665
The announcement comes just hours before

00:00:32.665 --> 00:00:33.433
markets open.

00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:33.933
And we're keeping

00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:34.968
a close eye on Wall

00:00:34.968 --> 00:00:36.903
Street to see how it reacts

00:00:36.903 --> 00:00:38.071
when the markets open.

00:00:38.071 --> 00:00:38.605
Our business

00:00:38.605 --> 00:00:39.139
and politics

00:00:39.139 --> 00:00:39.873
correspondent Vanessa

00:00:39.873 --> 00:00:41.007
Jurkovich, outside of a first year

00:00:41.007 --> 00:00:42.742
public bank here in New York City.

00:00:42.742 --> 00:00:44.911
Vanessa, good morning to you.

00:00:44.911 --> 00:00:47.580
What is so shocking is that 11 weeks ago,

00:00:47.881 --> 00:00:50.283
Jamie Dimon and JP Morgan led the way

00:00:50.283 --> 00:00:51.751
for all these big banks

00:00:51.751 --> 00:00:52.218
to inject

00:00:52.218 --> 00:00:54.220
$30 billion into First Republic

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:55.455
to try to save it.

00:00:55.455 --> 00:00:57.123
And even that didn't work.

00:00:59.959 --> 00:01:00.927
That's right, Poppy.

00:01:00.927 --> 00:01:02.128
And later this morning,

00:01:02.128 --> 00:01:02.762
when this four

00:01:02.762 --> 00:01:03.430
First Republic

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:04.230
branch behind me

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:06.399
and 83 others around the country open,

00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:08.134
it will be under new ownership.

00:01:08.134 --> 00:01:11.071
JPMorgan Chase buying First Republic Bank

00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:12.439
and a deal overnight

00:01:12.439 --> 00:01:15.108
announced this morning by the FDIC.

00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:17.010
This was an effort by regulators

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:17.877
to shore up

00:01:17.877 --> 00:01:20.580
consumer confidence in the banking system

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:22.315
as part of this deal.

00:01:22.315 --> 00:01:23.016
JP Morgan

00:01:23.016 --> 00:01:24.050
Chase will assume

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:27.320
all deposits of First Republic Bank.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:28.254
So when people

00:01:28.254 --> 00:01:30.023
go to the bank this morning,

00:01:30.023 --> 00:01:32.092
their deposits are safe.

00:01:32.092 --> 00:01:33.660
This all started to unfold

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.161
several weeks ago

00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:37.030
after the failure of SVB

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:38.398
and Signature Bank

00:01:38.398 --> 00:01:39.265
But as you mentioned,

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:40.667
there was that big cash

00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:44.871
infusion of $30 billion led by JP Morgan.

00:01:44.871 --> 00:01:46.239
But things really started

00:01:46.239 --> 00:01:47.774
to spiral last week.

00:01:47.774 --> 00:01:49.943
After the first quarter earnings call

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:52.112
by First Republic, where they revealed

00:01:52.378 --> 00:01:53.646
that deposits

00:01:53.646 --> 00:01:58.251
fell by 40% totaling about $100 billion.

00:01:58.418 --> 00:02:00.987
That sent stock prices falling

00:02:01.187 --> 00:02:02.222
just on Friday.

00:02:02.222 --> 00:02:05.391
Shares were trading at about $3, down

00:02:05.391 --> 00:02:09.963
from about $122 earlier in March.

00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:11.131
We do have a statement

00:02:11.131 --> 00:02:12.465
from the Treasury Department

00:02:12.465 --> 00:02:13.666
out early this morning

00:02:13.666 --> 00:02:14.834
reacting to the news.

00:02:14.834 --> 00:02:15.602
I will read that to you.

00:02:15.602 --> 00:02:16.669
Here's what they said.

00:02:16.669 --> 00:02:18.371
Quote, Treasury is encouraged

00:02:18.371 --> 00:02:20.540
that this institution was resolved

00:02:20.707 --> 00:02:22.242
with the least loss,

00:02:22.242 --> 00:02:22.642
the least

00:02:22.642 --> 00:02:24.544
cost to the deposit insurance fund

00:02:24.544 --> 00:02:25.311
and in a manner

00:02:25.311 --> 00:02:27.147
that protected all depositors

00:02:27.147 --> 00:02:28.281
The banking system remains

00:02:28.281 --> 00:02:29.649
sound and resilient,

00:02:29.649 --> 00:02:30.316
and Americans

00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:31.184
should feel confident

00:02:31.184 --> 00:02:32.852
in the safety of their deposits

00:02:32.852 --> 00:02:34.988
and the ability of the banking system

00:02:34.988 --> 00:02:36.923
to fulfill its essential function.

00:02:36.923 --> 00:02:38.224
Of providing credit

00:02:38.224 --> 00:02:39.926
to businesses and families.

00:02:39.926 --> 00:02:43.096
So clearly, this is an effort by the FDIC

00:02:43.229 --> 00:02:45.265
and offered by JPMorgan Chase

00:02:45.265 --> 00:02:47.367
and an effort by the Treasury Department

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:50.470
to assure customers and consumers that

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:51.271
the banking system

00:02:51.271 --> 00:02:52.438
here in the United States

00:02:52.438 --> 00:02:54.140
is, in fact, safe Poppy.

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:56.309
Still just stunning to see three big U.S.

00:02:56.309 --> 00:02:57.443
bank failures since March.

00:02:57.443 --> 00:02:58.211
Vanessa, thank you.

00:02:58.211 --> 00:02:59.212
We'll check back in

00:02:59.212 --> 00:03:00.280
with you throughout the morning.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:01.347
And Caitlin and Christine,

00:03:01.347 --> 00:03:01.847
I mean,

00:03:01.848 --> 00:03:02.482
the truth is

00:03:02.482 --> 00:03:03.183
the FDIC

00:03:03.183 --> 00:03:06.386
still is going to pay $13 billion here

00:03:07.053 --> 00:03:09.155
in terms of of the cost of it.

00:03:09.189 --> 00:03:10.790
Well, that's
what they've forecast, actually,

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:11.357
because we don't know

00:03:11.357 --> 00:03:12.592
what the ultimate costs will be.

00:03:12.592 --> 00:03:13.126
But they've said

00:03:13.126 --> 00:03:15.128
it could be about 13 billion.

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:15.962
Christine Romans,

00:03:15.962 --> 00:03:16.729
you're obviously our chief

00:03:16.729 --> 00:03:17.397
business correspondent.

00:03:17.397 --> 00:03:18.598
What do we know about the details

00:03:18.598 --> 00:03:19.532
of how all of this happened

00:03:19.532 --> 00:03:20.333
and what it looks like,

00:03:20.333 --> 00:03:21.034
what they've agreed to?

00:03:21.034 --> 00:03:22.602
It was just an amazing weekend.

00:03:22.602 --> 00:03:23.603
I got to tell you,

00:03:23.603 --> 00:03:25.104
I mean, this is JPMorgan

00:03:25.104 --> 00:03:26.739
taking over the assets

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:28.541
and the deposits of this bank.

00:03:28.541 --> 00:03:30.143
And then there's going to be a loss

00:03:30.143 --> 00:03:30.910
sharing provision

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:32.879
with the FDIC for anything that comes up

00:03:32.879 --> 00:03:34.681
that could be problematic

00:03:34.681 --> 00:03:35.582
on the bank's books.

00:03:35.582 --> 00:03:37.083
So that a little bit of a sweetener

00:03:37.083 --> 00:03:37.917
from the FDIC

00:03:37.917 --> 00:03:39.018
to make sure that JPMorgan

00:03:39.018 --> 00:03:39.819
would come to the table.

00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:42.088
We know
there was an auction of this bank.

00:03:42.088 --> 00:03:43.256
So over the weekend,

00:03:43.256 --> 00:03:44.090
you had all these banks

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:44.657
who were vying

00:03:44.657 --> 00:03:46.593
for pieces of First Republic,

00:03:46.593 --> 00:03:48.061
but it was maybe PNC,

00:03:48.061 --> 00:03:49.262
JP Morgan and others.

00:03:49.262 --> 00:03:50.230
By the end of you

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:51.331
know, by the end of last week,

00:03:51.331 --> 00:03:52.365
it became pretty clear

00:03:52.365 --> 00:03:53.266
that the FDIC was going

00:03:53.266 --> 00:03:54.167
to have to step in

00:03:54.167 --> 00:03:55.201
and take over this bank.

00:03:55.201 --> 00:03:56.035
It was not going to be able

00:03:56.035 --> 00:03:56.970
to stand alone.

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:58.104
And I think
if you're waking up this morning

00:03:58.104 --> 00:03:58.838
and you're looking at this,

00:03:58.838 --> 00:04:00.573
I was just talking with a top House

00:04:00.573 --> 00:04:02.875
Republican who deals with all of this,

00:04:02.875 --> 00:04:03.843
and he says essentially

00:04:03.843 --> 00:04:04.944
this is unfinished work

00:04:04.944 --> 00:04:07.146
from as we be weakened from what happened

00:04:07.146 --> 00:04:08.615
with Signature Valley Bank.

00:04:08.615 --> 00:04:10.350
Well, so Silicon Valley Bank. Yeah.

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:11.584
So let's talk about what happened there,

00:04:11.584 --> 00:04:13.386
because you had this bank,

00:04:13.386 --> 00:04:16.556
First Republic, lost 41% of its deposits.

00:04:16.556 --> 00:04:19.926
$100 billion walked out of the door after

00:04:20.526 --> 00:04:22.295
SBB and Signature Bank failed,

00:04:22.295 --> 00:04:23.129
because what happened

00:04:23.129 --> 00:04:24.230
was that showed that

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:26.199
these banks like First Republic,

00:04:26.199 --> 00:04:28.401
they have a lot of uninsured deposits.

00:04:28.401 --> 00:04:29.969
People didn't want to be sitting there

00:04:29.969 --> 00:04:31.204
with all of their money in the bank.

00:04:31.204 --> 00:04:33.439
More than $250,000 in a bank

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:35.341
with uninsured deposits.

00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:37.043
And so they walked out the front door

00:04:37.043 --> 00:04:38.711
and took their money to other banks.

00:04:38.711 --> 00:04:39.912
First Republic catered

00:04:39.912 --> 00:04:42.148
to wealthy clients on the coasts.

00:04:42.382 --> 00:04:44.417
It was known for its $5 million

00:04:44.417 --> 00:04:45.618
jumbo mortgages.

00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:47.420
So when you talk about Republicans

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:48.721
in the House, look, nobody's

00:04:48.721 --> 00:04:50.223
crying over First Republic Bank,

00:04:50.223 --> 00:04:51.991
but you don't want chaos and turmoil

00:04:51.991 --> 00:04:52.425
to spread

00:04:52.425 --> 00:04:54.193
to the rest of the banking system

00:04:54.193 --> 00:04:54.927
as well here.

00:04:54.927 --> 00:04:55.528
And do we think

00:04:55.528 --> 00:04:57.063
that there's going to be chaos

00:04:57.063 --> 00:04:58.231
in the banking system

00:04:58.231 --> 00:04:58.898
as a result of this?

00:04:58.898 --> 00:04:59.565
So I've been talking

00:04:59.565 --> 00:05:00.767
to a lot of people over the weekend,

00:05:00.767 --> 00:05:01.768
and they think you've called

00:05:01.768 --> 00:05:03.236
this unfinished business

00:05:03.236 --> 00:05:05.405
They think that what happened with

00:05:05.405 --> 00:05:05.972
all that money,

00:05:05.972 --> 00:05:07.140
that was the lifeline

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:08.241
that went six weeks ago

00:05:08.241 --> 00:05:10.043
that went into the $30 billion

00:05:10.043 --> 00:05:10.910
that bought it

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:12.111
some time, know

00:05:12.111 --> 00:05:12.979
calmed down

00:05:12.979 --> 00:05:14.514
market fears, it bought it

00:05:14.514 --> 00:05:15.014
some time

00:05:15.014 --> 00:05:16.082
and allowed the industry

00:05:16.082 --> 00:05:18.151
to be able to find a way

00:05:18.151 --> 00:05:19.485
to manage this problem.

00:05:19.485 --> 00:05:20.920
Will there be more banks?

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:22.288
No one knows for sure.

00:05:22.288 --> 00:05:23.856
I mean, three big bank failures in

00:05:23.856 --> 00:05:24.691
just a couple of months

00:05:24.691 --> 00:05:26.326
is really remarkable.

00:05:26.326 --> 00:05:28.461
But for the most part,

00:05:28.461 --> 00:05:30.630
most banks are perfectly healthy

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:31.531
and will be able to manage

00:05:31.531 --> 00:05:32.498
this interest rate risk.

00:05:32.498 --> 00:05:33.232
But can we talk about

00:05:33.232 --> 00:05:34.334
the FDIC report here?

00:05:34.334 --> 00:05:35.535
Because I'm obsessed with this.

00:05:35.535 --> 00:05:36.703
This is an aspect of this, you know,

00:05:36.703 --> 00:05:38.004
talking about the root causes of here

00:05:38.004 --> 00:05:38.671
and what went down.

00:05:38.671 --> 00:05:39.505
The FDIC report

00:05:39.505 --> 00:05:40.773
basically said that

00:05:40.773 --> 00:05:41.174
because they have

00:05:41.174 --> 00:05:42.709
so many staffing shortages,

00:05:42.709 --> 00:05:44.177
that led to a part of what

00:05:44.177 --> 00:05:45.244
we've been seeing playing out

00:05:45.244 --> 00:05:45.945
with these banks.

00:05:45.945 --> 00:05:47.013
Yeah, that Fed report on

00:05:47.013 --> 00:05:47.980
the collapse of SDB

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:49.415
was really interesting, in part

00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:51.784
because the Fed is both the regulator

00:05:51.784 --> 00:05:54.187
and the supervisor and basically said

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:55.688
there were all these open

00:05:55.688 --> 00:05:58.224
red flags at Silicon Valley Bank

00:05:58.591 --> 00:05:59.726
that were not followed up on

00:05:59.726 --> 00:06:02.028
and that management didn't understand

00:06:02.161 --> 00:06:02.895
what was going on.

00:06:02.895 --> 00:06:04.497
But also just poor bank

00:06:04.497 --> 00:06:05.732
management and

00:06:05.732 --> 00:06:07.367
oversight was not as robust

00:06:07.367 --> 00:06:08.234
as it should have been.

00:06:08.234 --> 00:06:09.969
When I sat down with JPMorgan CEO

00:06:09.969 --> 00:06:11.404
Jamie Dimon just last month,

00:06:11.404 --> 00:06:13.239
I asked him about these string

00:06:13.239 --> 00:06:14.941
of bank failures. Would there be more?

00:06:14.941 --> 00:06:15.908
Here's what he said. Ben

00:06:18.144 --> 00:06:20.146
This is not 2008.

00:06:20.146 --> 00:06:22.148
Okay. This is much more limited.

00:06:22.148 --> 00:06:23.383
There are only a handful of banks

00:06:23.383 --> 00:06:25.585
that have this particular problem.

00:06:25.585 --> 00:06:26.986
They'll eventually be resolved

00:06:26.986 --> 00:06:28.121
one way or another.

00:06:28.121 --> 00:06:28.654
And I think

00:06:28.654 --> 00:06:30.056
people should take a deep breath

00:06:30.056 --> 00:06:31.591
in a week or two.

00:06:31.591 --> 00:06:32.225
A lot of these banks

00:06:32.225 --> 00:06:33.292
have been reporting earnings.

00:06:33.292 --> 00:06:35.828
I think they're probably pretty good.

00:06:35.828 --> 00:06:37.397
What we subsequently learned

00:06:37.397 --> 00:06:38.965
as we bring in Roger Altman, former

00:06:38.965 --> 00:06:40.533
deputy secretary of the treasury

00:06:40.533 --> 00:06:41.434
and founder and senior

00:06:41.434 --> 00:06:42.969
chairman of Evercore. Roger, thank you.

00:06:42.969 --> 00:06:43.703
What we learned

00:06:43.703 --> 00:06:45.171
when those bank earnings came in

00:06:45.171 --> 00:06:46.506
is how much money fled

00:06:46.506 --> 00:06:48.541
from First Republic in the first quarter.

00:06:48.708 --> 00:06:49.342
But it's notable

00:06:49.342 --> 00:06:50.176
because Jamie Dimon,

00:06:50.176 --> 00:06:51.110
who's now leading the bank

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:51.844
that is buying up

00:06:51.844 --> 00:06:52.912
most of their assets,

00:06:52.912 --> 00:06:54.781
is the one who led the initial attempt

00:06:54.781 --> 00:06:55.681
to rescue.

00:06:55.681 --> 00:06:56.449
He's the one who picked up

00:06:56.449 --> 00:06:57.583
the phone, called the other bank

00:06:57.583 --> 00:06:58.418
or said, let's get in here.

00:06:58.418 --> 00:07:00.520
Let's try to rescue First Republic.

00:07:00.686 --> 00:07:02.455
I wonder why it didn't work.

00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:04.524
I think it didn't

00:07:04.524 --> 00:07:07.627
work because the deposits kept leaving

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:09.028
First Republic.

00:07:09.028 --> 00:07:10.930
What basically happened here

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:12.465
is that after the failure of Silicon

00:07:12.465 --> 00:07:14.400
Valley Bank a few weeks ago,

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:16.736
a lot of depositors just simply decided

00:07:17.103 --> 00:07:18.704
that they didn't want their money

00:07:18.704 --> 00:07:20.573
and Medium-Sized Banks,

00:07:20.573 --> 00:07:22.542
and they moved that money

00:07:22.542 --> 00:07:24.076
either to the very largest banks

00:07:24.076 --> 00:07:24.911
like JP Morgan

00:07:24.911 --> 00:07:26.012
or Bank of America

00:07:26.012 --> 00:07:28.748
or to money market funds

00:07:28.748 --> 00:07:31.517
and First Republic in recent weeks lost

00:07:31.717 --> 00:07:33.686
almost all of its deposits.

00:07:33.686 --> 00:07:34.620
And what happens is

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:36.622
they had to replace those deposits

00:07:36.622 --> 00:07:38.057
with a higher cost

00:07:38.057 --> 00:07:39.192
and loans from the Federal Home

00:07:39.192 --> 00:07:41.227
Loan Bank Board and from the Fed.

00:07:41.227 --> 00:07:42.962
And that crushed their earnings,

00:07:42.962 --> 00:07:44.530
that crushed their share price.

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:46.199
And it's snowball downhill.

00:07:46.199 --> 00:07:47.300
If you're waking up this morning

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:49.202
and you have your deposits at

00:07:49.202 --> 00:07:50.436
first public,

00:07:50.436 --> 00:07:52.505
if you have a mortgage to first republic,

00:07:52.505 --> 00:07:53.973
if you pay your small business

00:07:53.973 --> 00:07:55.374
payroll through First Republic,

00:07:55.374 --> 00:07:56.175
what do you need to know?

00:07:56.175 --> 00:07:57.343
Your fine your fine.

00:07:57.343 --> 00:07:58.344
In fact, I find, in fact,

00:07:59.312 --> 00:08:00.746
all depositors

00:08:00.746 --> 00:08:01.581
in the United States

00:08:01.581 --> 00:08:02.849
today are fine,

00:08:02.849 --> 00:08:05.017
apparently up to any ceiling because

